.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt PIE-MAKEFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pie-makefs
.Nd create a ZFS pool image from a directory tree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl D
.Op Fl x
.Op Fl t Cm zfs
.Op Fl F Ar mtree-specfile
.Op Fl M Ar minimum-size
.Op Fl m Ar maximum-size
.Op Fl o Ar zfs-options
.Op Fl s Ar image-size
.Op Fl T Ar timestamp
.Ar image-file
.Ar directory | manifest
.Op Ar extra-directory ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is FreeBSD's
.Xr makefs 8
with only its zfs writer, for Linux.
It writes
.Ar image-file ,
a pool on one disk vdev, from the files in
.Ar directory
and any
.Ar extra-directory ,
or from the files an mtree
.Ar manifest
names.
It needs no ZFS kernel module.
Regular files, directories and symbolic links are written.
Device nodes and FIFOs are left out with a warning, and sockets are left
out.
Owners, modes and times come from the tree, so run it under
.Xr fakeroot 1
to give files other owners.
The same tree, options and
.Fl T
time give the same image when
.Ev LC_ALL
is
.Dv C .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl D
Warn about duplicate paths in an mtree manifest instead of failing.
Given twice, say nothing about them.
.It Fl F Ar mtree-specfile
Take owners, modes and other attributes from an mtree specification.
Owners and groups must be numeric, uid= and gid=, because names need
.Fl N .
File flags other than
.Cm none
are refused.
A time= in the specification sets the access and modification times,
over
.Fl T ,
and the change time stays the
.Fl T
time.
.It Fl M Ar minimum-size , Fl m Ar maximum-size
Bound the image's size.
.Fl M
alone is not enough: the image needs
.Fl m
or
.Fl s .
.It Fl o Ar zfs-options
A comma-separated list of the options below.
.It Fl s Ar image-size
The image's size, with an optional k, m, g or t suffix.
It sets both bounds.
.Fl s
or
.Fl m
is required.
.It Fl T Ar timestamp
Give every file this time, in seconds since the epoch, or the
modification and change times of the file it names.
A file with the number as its name takes the number's place.
For the same image from the same tree, give a number, such as
.Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH .
.It Fl t Cm zfs
The file system type.
zfs is the default and the only one built.
It must come before any
.Fl o ,
because it discards the options given before it.
.It Fl x
Leave out files that the specification does not name.
.El
.Pp
The zfs options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm poolname Ns = Ns Ar name
The pool's name.
It is required.
.It Cm rootpath Ns = Ns Ar path
A prefix for the datasets' mount points,
.Pa / Ns Ar poolname
by default.
A bootable pool uses
.Pa / .
One dataset's mount point must be the prefix itself.
.It Cm bootfs Ns = Ns Ar dataset
The pool's bootfs property.
.It Cm fs Ns = Ns Ar dataset Ns Op ; Ns Ar property Ns = Ns Ar value ...
Make a dataset, with some of its properties: atime, canmount,
compression, exec, mountpoint and setuid.
.It Cm ashift Ns = Ns Ar n
The base-2 logarithm of the smallest block size, from 9 to 14, and 12
by default.
.It Cm mssize Ns = Ns Ar size
The size of each metaslab.
.It Cm poolguid Ns = Ns Ar guid
The pool's GUID.
Without it, every pool gets the same GUID, and two such pools can't be
imported on one system.
.It Cm verify-txgs
Make ZFS verify the pool's metadata when it imports it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
After a failure,
.Ar image-file
may be incomplete and should be removed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write a pool named pie with one dataset as its root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
LC_ALL=C pie-makefs -t zfs -T 1700000000 -s 1g \e
    -o poolname=pie -o rootpath=/ -o bootfs=pie/sys/default \e
    -o 'fs=pie;canmount=off;mountpoint=none' \e
    -o 'fs=pie/sys;canmount=off;mountpoint=none' \e
    -o 'fs=pie/sys/default;mountpoint=/' pool.img root
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fakeroot 1 ,
.Xr makefs 8 ,
.Xr zdb 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The cd9660, ffs and msdos writers are not built.
.Fl N
is not supported.
.Pp
A hard link must be in the same dataset as its file, and each dataset
needs its own mount point.
A symbolic link's target may have at most 144 bytes.
A tree about a thousand levels deep overflows the stack.
.Pp
The mountpoint property must be a path or none, not legacy, and readonly
can't be set.
compression is recorded, but the data is written uncompressed, and sparse
files are written in full.
